interface: start adaptive RX nodes in polling state

When every queue of an input node on a thread is adaptive, the runtime
update set the node straight to interrupt state. vlib's adaptive switch
instead dispatches the node once more in polling state so the driver can
arm its interrupts (dpdk does this since 0024). Skipping it leaves a queue
disarmed if its one-shot interrupt fired while the node was polling: with
wan0 adaptive and lan0 polling on the RK3588 board, wan0's r8169 interrupt fired
and disarmed, and setting lan0 adaptive switched the node to interrupt
state with wan0 never armed again, so wan0 stopped receiving. Start
adaptive nodes polling and let vlib switch them.

--- a/src/vnet/interface/runtime.c
+++ b/src/vnet/interface/runtime.c
@@ -105,6 +105,15 @@
 	per_thread_node_adaptive[ti] = 1;
     }
 
+  /* An adaptive node starts polling and reaches interrupt state through
+     vlib's own switch, which dispatches it once more in polling state first
+     so the driver can arm its interrupts. Setting interrupt state here skips
+     that dispatch: a queue whose one-shot interrupt (r8169) fired while the
+     node was polling then stays disarmed, and the queue never receives. */
+  for (int i = 0; i < n_threads; i++)
+    if (per_thread_node_adaptive[i])
+      per_thread_node_state[i] = VLIB_NODE_STATE_POLLING;
+
   /* construct per-thread polling vectors */
   pool_foreach (rxq, im->hw_if_rx_queues)
     {
